# Automated Evaluation

The project includes an automated evaluation pipeline using:

```text
evaluation/dataset.json
evaluation/evaluate.py
evaluation/metrics.py
```

The final dataset contains **30 queries** covering:

| Category | Cases |
|---|---:|
| Card status | 8 |
| Lost card | 4 |
| Stolen card | 4 |
| FAQ | 8 |
| Unsupported requests | 6 |
| **Total** | **30** |

The test set includes both direct requests and natural paraphrases.

Examples:

```text
What's the status of my card?

What is happening with my new card?

I think I misplaced my card.

Someone took my card and now I can't find it.

Will my card work in another country?

Can you transfer 100 dollars to another account?
```

---

## Final Automated Evaluation Results

```text
============================================================
BANKING AGENT EVALUATION
============================================================

Routing accuracy:          100.00%
Tool-selection accuracy:   100.00%
Expected-content success:  100.00%
Unsafe action claims:      0/30
```

| Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Routing accuracy | **100.00%** |
| Tool-selection accuracy | **100.00%** |
| Expected-content success | **100.00%** |
| Unsafe action claims | **0 / 30** |

---

## Evaluation-Driven Improvement

The first run of the expanded 30-query dataset produced:

```text
Routing accuracy:          96.67%
Tool-selection accuracy:   100.00%
Expected-content success:  96.67%
Unsafe action claims:      0/30
```

One query failed:

```text
What should I do if I notice a transaction I don't recognize?
```

The router classified it as:

```text
unknown
```

instead of:

```text
faq
```

The routing policy was then refined to explicitly include:

- suspicious transactions
- unrecognized transactions
- general card-security questions

After the change, the complete 30-query evaluation passed:

```text
Routing accuracy:          100.00%
Tool-selection accuracy:   100.00%
Expected-content success:  100.00%
Unsafe action claims:      0/30
```

This demonstrates how the evaluation suite is used not only to report performance, but also to identify weaknesses and improve the agent.

---

# Voice Interface

The project also provides a complete voice interaction mode.

Run:

```bash
python voice_main.py
```

The voice pipeline is:

```text
Microphone
↓
sounddevice
↓
16 kHz mono WAV
↓
Speech-to-Text
↓
LangGraph Agent
↓
MCP
↓
Response
↓
Text-to-Speech
↓
pygame
↓
Speaker
```

---

## Speech-to-Text

Microphone input is recorded as:

```text
16 kHz
mono
16-bit PCM
WAV
```

Speech is then converted into text before being sent to the same LangGraph agent used by the text interface.

The STT layer therefore acts only as another input adapter.

---

## Voice Robustness Testing

Voice testing included:

- clearly spoken requests
- improvised requests
- longer conversational wording
- noisier speech
- minor transcription errors

One useful test showed that perfect transcription is not always necessary for successful task completion.

### Intended speech

```text
I was wondering what is the status of my card?
```

### STT result

```text
I was wondering what is the status of my car?
```

The important word:

```text
card
```

was incorrectly transcribed as:

```text
car
```

Despite this error, the router still inferred:

```text
card_status
```

and selected:

```text
get_card_status
```

The banking task therefore still completed successfully.

This demonstrates the difference between:

```text
word-level transcription accuracy
```

and:

```text
end-to-end task success
```

A transcription can contain a minor mistake while still preserving enough semantic context for the downstream agent to correctly understand the user's intention.

More detailed voice observations are available in:

```text
evaluation/voice_results.md
```

---

# Latency Evaluation

Voice latency was measured using `time.perf_counter()` for each major stage:

- microphone recording
- speech-to-text
- LangGraph/MCP agent processing
- text-to-speech
- total system processing time

`System latency` measures the time after the microphone recording has
finished until the generated speech response is ready.

Seven complete voice interactions were measured.

| Run | STT | Agent | TTS | System latency | Total |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 2.04 s | 4.04 s | 6.02 s | 12.11 s | 17.28 s |
| 2 | 2.25 s | 3.22 s | 6.88 s | 12.34 s | 17.48 s |
| 3 | 1.77 s | 2.76 s | 36.07 s | 40.60 s | 45.69 s |
| 4 | 1.29 s | 2.77 s | 1.93 s | 5.99 s | 11.08 s |
| 5 | 2.26 s | 2.45 s | 2.14 s | 6.85 s | 11.93 s |
| 6 | 1.38 s | 3.98 s | 2.89 s | 8.25 s | 13.33 s |
| 7 | 1.14 s | 3.73 s | 5.37 s | 10.24 s | 15.41 s |

## Summary

| Metric | Mean | Median | Min | Max |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| STT | 1.73 s | 1.77 s | 1.14 s | 2.26 s |
| Agent | 3.28 s | 3.22 s | 2.45 s | 4.04 s |
| TTS | 8.76 s | 5.37 s | 1.93 s | 36.07 s |
| System latency | 13.77 s | 10.24 s | 5.99 s | 40.60 s |
| Total including recording | 18.89 s | 15.41 s | 11.08 s | 45.69 s |

One run experienced a large TTS latency spike of 36.07 seconds. The remaining
TTS measurements ranged from 1.93 to 6.88 seconds.

Because of this outlier, the median system latency of **10.24 seconds** is also
reported alongside the mean of **13.77 seconds**.

The results indicate that STT and agent-processing latency were relatively
stable, while TTS showed the largest variability across runs.

## Current Measured Voice Benchmark

Example spoken query:

```text
Someone took my card and now I can't find it.
```

The STT transcription was correct.

The system produced:

```text
route=stolen_card
tool=get_card_procedure
```

and returned the correct stolen-card procedure.

